  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 340 AM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nThe next upstream short wave moves into the Upper Midwest Thursday \nnight, and then southeast into Iowa and northern Illinois on Friday.\nThis morning's model runs aren't in as good agreement with the \nstrength and speed of the wave as it moves across the Midwest into \nthe Ohio Valley, and this translates into position, speed, and \nstrength differences with the low level reflection of the wave. \nHowever, the model consensus supports increasing PoPs late Thursday \nnight into Friday morning, and then continuing precip chances \nthrough Saturday.  The GFS and ECMWF both show enough instability \nfor thunderstorms Friday ahead of the surface low, with up to 30-\n35kts of shear.  Could therefore see some organized convection \nFriday with a damaging wind threat if there's enough instability. \nHowever that's currently questionable as convective debris and \nongoing precipitation will probably limit daytime heating.  Mean \nprecipitable water values will be around 2 inches over our area on \nFriday according to the latest run of the LREF. This is around the \n95th percentile of climatology, so some locally heavy rain will be \npossible, though the storms look like they should be progressive.\n\nModels continue to print out light QPF Friday night and Saturday as \nthe short wave moves across the area and low level moisture wraps \nback around the system.  Differences in the speed of the short wave \ncontinue, and therefore timing the end of the precip chances is \nuncertain. Many of the the time-lagged ECMWF ensemble members in the \nlatest LREF hang on to precip Saturday night into Sunday due to the \nEC's slower solution.  Some solutions even hang on to precip chances \nMonday and Tuesday afternoon across southeast Missouri into southern \nIllinois as the short wave lingers over the lower Ohio/Tennessee \nValley.  However the GEFS and GEPS members mostly dry us out by \nSaturday evening.  Confidence in the precip forecast is low beyond \nSaturday with all this in mind.  Temperatures through the period \nwill remain near to a few degrees below normal.  The coolest day \nwill likely be Saturday with clouds and showers with the surface \nlow east of the area over eastern Illinois or northern Kentucky.",
